I have the same issue with this statement that I did in https://github.com/openssl/openssl/pull/29897
1) It has the same problem that the above issue does, in that its not required to unload providers prior to calling OSSL_LIB_CTX_free (unless you can point to a reproducer that indicates something goes wrong with that use case)
2) while its not allowed to call OSSL_PROVIDER_unload on a provider reference after calling OSSL_LIB_CTX_free() on the context that loaded the provider, that is the defintion of a use-after-free case, as the provider pointer is no longer valid (due to the automatic cleanup in (1) above).  Its no different than:
```
char *new = malloc(4);
free(new);
sprintf(new, "b");
```

If you want to warn against generalized use after free cases, it would probably be better to just document the fact that providers are subordonate objects to the libctx from which they were allocated, and so freeing a libctx automatically frees any providers loaded against that libctx and so should no longer be referenced after the free call.

