this isn't true.

OSSL_PROVIDER_unload() is defined as follows:
```
int OSSL_PROVIDER_unload(OSSL_PROVIDER *prov)
{
    if (!ossl_provider_deactivate(prov, 1))
        return 0;
    ossl_provider_free(prov);
    return 1;
}
```

if you follow the code path for OSSL_LIB_CTX_free:
```
OSSL_LIB_CTX_free -> 
 context_deinit ->
  context_deinit_objs ->
   ossl_provider_store_free ->
     sk_OSSL_PROVIDER_pop_free ->
       provider_deactivate_free -
```

Where provider_deactivate_free is defined as:
```
static void provider_deactivate_free(OSSL_PROVIDER *prov)
{
    if (prov->flag_activated)
        ossl_provider_deactivate(prov, 1);
    ossl_provider_free(prov);
}
```

So freeing the libctx in which you have loaded a set of providers automatically tears down and frees providers in exactly the same way that OSSL_PROVIDER_unload() does.  Its automatic. 

You mentioned in your initial comment that there was a use after free case here.  Do you have some reproducer of that occurring?      That seems like a legitimate bug to fix, but we shouldn't document that you have to unload providers prior to freeing a library context when the code is written to do it for you.

